course: dictionary
lesson: 7508
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word potter can be a person, an action, or a name. «When farms made more food than needed, some people could have other jobs, such as potter, builder, priest or leader.» Here a potter is a person with a job: someone who makes things from clay.
explain: Now read this: «Grandad likes to potter in the garden on Sunday.» There is no clay here. The clues are garden and Sunday. In British English, to potter means to do small, pleasant jobs slowly and happily. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence, then try it in place of the hard word.
explain: Potter with a capital P is a name, not a job. Beatrix Potter wrote Peter Rabbit, and Harry Potter is a made-up wizard. When a word starts with a capital letter in the middle of a sentence, it is often a name.
fact: A potter is a person who makes pots, bowls and other things from clay.
fact: Potters often use a spinning wheel, and they bake their clay in a kiln, which is a very hot oven.
fact: «Wheels appeared about 5,500 years ago; potters used spinning wheels, and soon wheels were put on carts.»
fact: In UK, Irish and Australian English, «to potter» means to do small, pleasant jobs or to move about slowly and happily.
fact: Potter with a capital P is a surname, as in Beatrix Potter and the fictional Harry Potter.
exercise: «When farms made more food than needed, some people could have other jobs, such as potter, builder, priest or leader.» What is a potter in this sentence? | *A person who makes pots and bowls from clay | A very hot oven for baking | A person who does small jobs slowly in the garden | A person who writes stories for children
exercise: «Grandad likes to potter in the garden on Sunday.» What does potter mean here? | To shout loudly at the weeds | To make pots from clay in a kiln | To run very fast | *To do small, pleasant jobs slowly
exercise: Which sentence uses Potter as a name? | The potter threw the clay on the wheel | Potters bake clay in a kiln | *Beatrix Potter wrote Peter Rabbit for children | Grandad likes to potter about on Sunday
test: «Very thick lumps of clay can trap water or air that turns to steam in the kiln, a very hot oven, and crack the piece.» Why do potters keep their walls even and not too thick? | *To stop the pot cracking in the kiln | To make the pot heavier to carry | To make the clay turn to steam | To make the kiln hotter
test: What do potters bake their clay in? | A garden shed | A cart with wheels | *A kiln, a very hot oven | A rabbit hole
exam: «The old man likes to potter by the river each morning.» Which meaning fits best? | A person who writes books | *To move about slowly and happily | To make pots and bowls from clay | A very hot oven
